 ```
 
 Some migration and gateway plugin tests require Bun on PATH and use local plugin fixtures; they skip when Bun is unavailable. Report any skipped tests and verify the community package separately when its implementation changes.
+
+## Opt-in clean removal
+
+An npm provider plugin can declare `magpie.cleanRemove.providers` in its package manifest. This is an opt-in lifecycle extension: existing plugins retain their removal behavior. `store.go` persists the provider ids in the entry, so a partially removed package still has enough ownership information to retry. `cleanremove.go` validates the provider ids and coordinates disposal and package-cache cleanup.
+
+The host gives `input.magpie.cleanRemove: 1` and the effective account-scoped `input.magpie.proxyFor(origin)` to native transports. Before removal it validates that the listed ids are not shared with another loaded plugin, waits for `magpie.uninstall` in the active and retiring host generations, and clears those sign-ins. On ordinary host exit it calls `magpie.dispose`, which frees resources while preserving sign-ins.
+
+Opt-in removal clears the plugin config provider entries, provider/model cache and registration. Explicit listings and background refreshes both check the cache epoch before publishing, so a response begun before removal cannot restore the deleted catalog. npm removal also deletes package-owned Bun cache entries by package name, including hashed manifest indexes whose serialized package name is checked; local source directories and shared runtime files are preserved. Bun manifest format v0.0.7 is supported; unfamiliar or corrupt formats fail removal rather than promise a clean cache. `provider/plugin_cleanremove.go` clears plugin account display metadata and provider overrides. A corrupt account store or a failed disposal is an error, not a successful uninstall; the registration remains available for retry. These operations are not an atomic transaction across every file, so a failure may have already stopped the backend or cleared some owned state.
+
+Git download caches, user-created routing groups, saved selections, request history, backups and exports remain under their respective owners. The extension does not promise erasure of all historical references. `cleanremove_test.go` covers disposal, package/cache/account/catalog removal, failure and shared-provider isolation; `plugin_cleanremove_test.go` covers account metadata and unrelated custom providers.
